From: gameOS
Subject: [PATCH] ARM64 JIT: block linking, host flags, finer invalidation

Performance work on the ARM64 JIT, adapted to this melonDS 0.9.3 tree from
the GPL-3.0+ melonDS performance patch series.

  1. Block linking: an exit with a known target goes on into the next block
     instead of returning to ARM_Dispatch.  The first pass through an exit
     slot learns the target (melonds_jit_link) and ARM_Dispatch patches the
     slot's B straight to the next block's entry; UnlinkBlock restores the
     slot when the block retires or is invalidated.  MELONDS_JIT_NOLINK=1
     disables linking at runtime (for A/B runs).

  2. Host flags: guest NZCV is tracked in the host's condition flags
     (HostFlagsEpoch/HostFlagsMask); conditional instructions branch on the
     host NZCV through condFlags[] instead of rebuilding the flags, MRS
     rebuilds them on demand.  InvalidateHostFlags on MSR and on fastmem
     patches that may become calls.

  3. InvalidateByAddr only drops the blocks that overlap the written
     16 bytes (MELONDS_JIT_INVAL16=0 restores the old 512-byte behaviour
     for A/B runs).

Measured with a static libretro bench harness under qemu-user (Pokemon
White 2, 600 frames, warmup 120, threaded renderer off): 66.7 fps before,
103.5 fps after (1.55x), identical video/audio hashes.  A PGO pass on top
adds another ~9% (separate build).

